  </section>

  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Who is responsible for cluster mailboxes in an HOA?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "In most private communities the association owns and maintains the cluster box unit, because the developer purchased and installed it and conveyed it to the association at turnover. The Postal Service approves the location and controls the carrier's arrow lock, but does not maintain privately installed units. Older neighborhoods where USPS installed the boxes directly are the main exception, so confirm ownership with your local postmaster and your turnover documents."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does USPS repair a broken cluster mailbox?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Generally no. If the association owns the unit, the association pays for repairs and replacement. The Postal Service maintains only the arrow lock the carrier uses for delivery. USPS can also suspend delivery to a unit it considers unsafe or non-compliant, which makes prompt repair the association's problem to solve."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Who pays to replace a lost cluster mailbox key?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The resident normally pays. Because USPS will not rekey a privately owned unit, the association or its management company arranges the lock and key replacement and charges a published fee. Routing the work through management keeps the hardware uniform and creates a record."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What should a board do after cluster mailbox theft?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Run two tracks at once. Affected residents report the mail theft to the U.S. Postal Inspection Service and local police, since mail theft is a federal offense. Separately, the association documents the damage, secures and repairs the unit, notifies its insurer if the loss exceeds the deductible, and reviews lighting, sightlines, and camera coverage if the same location is targeted repeatedly."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Should cluster mailboxes be in the reserve study?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. Cluster box units are an association-owned site component with a finite useful life, and replacement must use a current USPS-approved model, which can change the footprint and the cost. Carrying them as a discrete reserve component rather than inside a general site-amenities line avoids funding replacement through a special assessment."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>
`;
